\section{Read the joint matrix}{
Rows and columns are named by parameter:
\itemize{
\item \verb{log_scale:<covariate>}: \code{log(sigma / u)}, the natural log of that
covariate's sigma after dividing it by the internal distance unit
\code{u = object$kernel_inputs$unit_conv}. Dividing by \code{u} shifts the log by a
constant, so the variance equals the variance of log(sigma) in map units.
\item \verb{log_shape:<covariate>}: the log shape parameter, for \code{kernel = "expow"}.
\item \verb{beta:<term>}: a regression coefficient on the link scale, named after
its model-matrix column, for example \code{beta:(Intercept)}. A standardized
kernel-weighted covariate's coefficient describes one standard deviation
of that covariate.
\item A final dispersion row when the family has one: \code{log_theta} for a
negative binomial model and \code{log_residual_sd} for a Gaussian model.
}

The square root of a diagonal element is a standard error on that
parameter's scale. For sigma, a 95\% Wald interval in map units is
\verb{sigma x exp(-1.96 x SE)} to \verb{sigma x exp(1.96 x SE)}, where \code{SE} is the
standard error of \code{log_scale}. These are the limits that \code{summary(object)}
reports, and \code{object$scale_est$SE} is \verb{sigma x SE}. Off-diagonal elements
show how the estimates trade off, and \code{\link[stats:cov2cor]{stats::cov2cor()}} turns them into
correlations.

Joint covariance is unavailable for fits at a parameter bound or when the
identifiability checks fail. In that case \code{type = "joint"} raises an error
that lists \code{object$uncertainty$reasons}, rather than reporting a misleading
Wald result. The conditional matrix remains available.
